# __APP_NAME__

Run `cargo run` in this directory to start the Connect server at the address in `app.json`. In a second shell, use `node client.mjs` to read the public service setting. The response comes from the deployment configuration.

Edit the entity in `proto/app/setting.proto` and run `paydirt gen` to regenerate the standard service contract and Rust bindings. Add domain RPCs in `proto/ext/app/setting_svc.ext.proto`. Implement only the methods you override on `SettingServiceLayer` in `src/core.rs`. Other methods pass to the next service, including the config-backed Get in `src/sink.rs`. The app owns these files and assembles the layers in `src/lib.rs`. Use `paydirt gen --check` in CI.

The native server creates its tables in `paydirt.sqlite3` by default. Set `DATABASE_URL` to a PostgreSQL URL to use that backend. For a tenanted entity, provision a tenant using the local app binary and convert the response's base64 ID to a 32-character hex string and put it in the `PAYDIRT_PRINCIPALS` token-to-tenant JSON map. Only the operator with database access can invoke the binary's internal commands. The public Connect services resolve their caller from the bearer token.

`cargo run -- app.Tenant Add '{"alias":"my-team"}'` prints the new tenant with its protobuf JSON (base64) ID. App-owned entities declared in `proto/` receive a generated Diesel sink and registration in the app router. Customize a service by wrapping its typed next service in the app's `storage::Layers` implementation.

## Browser sandbox

Run `paydirt sandbox init`, install Rust's `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target, Clang and `wasm-bindgen-cli` 0.2.130. In `sandbox/`, run `npm ci`, `npm run build:wasm`, and `npm run dev`. The page calls the same app router over a MessagePort. The database Worker owns SQLite and retains rows in OPFS across page reloads.

A web client can import `openSandbox(principals)` from `sandbox/client.js`, pass `box.link.transport` to its generated Connect client, then call `box.close()` on shutdown. Supply a map of opaque bearer tokens to hex tenant IDs to provision the local sandbox. The link implementation and its Apache license come from [Payday](https://github.com/lesomnus/payday/blob/53acc135/ts/src/sandbox/link.ts).

## Entity commands

Run `paydirt entity add --watch Gauge .`, edit the resulting entity, and run `paydirt gen`. The new declaration supplies the service, table and application commands. `paydirt doctor` checks generated files and compiles the application entry points. Two consecutive additions choose different domains even before generation.

Run `cargo run -- gauge --help` for the verbs its schema declares. The lowercase commands connect to `PAYDIRT_URL` and use `PAYDIRT_TOKEN` as their bearer credential. For example, `cargo run -- gauge ls --size 10 -o json` reads one page. Requests can also be JSON arguments or read from stdin with `-`. Use `-o protojson` for protobuf JSON identifiers, `-o prototext` for protobuf text and `-o name` for an identifier per line. For example, `-o 'template={{.id}} {{.alias}}'` prints two fields. Request bodies support `--in prototext` as well as JSON. The transport closes general Patch and Apply by default. A deployment that serves them opts in with `"allow_general_writes": true` in app.json.


## App-owned behavior

Declare a domain operation in `proto/ext/`, run `paydirt gen`, then implement that method on the generated service layer and mount it in `src/lib.rs`. The request passes through Gate, Audit, your Core, and the storage Sink. Use the typed next service with the incoming request context to keep related changes in the same scoped transaction. Service overlays and application Rust sources are preserved on regeneration. A failed write rolls back the transaction, including its audit trail and outbox records.

The server reads `PAYDIRT_PRINCIPALS` when it starts. A simple tenant credential uses a JSON map such as `{"dev-token":"<32 hex digits>"}`. Set `PAYDIRT_TOKEN=dev-token` on a client to use that credential. Deployments can also supply a role policy with `PAYDIRT_POLICY_FILE` and select an account membership using the header configured by `PAYDIRT_ACCOUNT_HEADER`.

For an RPC declared with `(payday.method).account`, derive a context with `database.account_reads(ctx, "/app.InvitationService/Accept", reads)` before calling another entity's typed Get or List. Build the scope with `AccountReads::default().allow("app.Invitation", ReadFilter::AccountField("recipient".into()))`. A second declaration can select specific IDs with `ReadFilter::Ids`. Each derived context retains its own scope. Undeclared entities return an empty list or NotFound. A call served as a tenant membership uses its tenant scope.

## Native background workers

Register application workers in the `workers` function in `src/lib.rs`. Each named worker receives a stop token. Choose `Placement::Everywhere` or `Placement::Singleton`. Set `"worker_lease":"postgres"` in `app.json` to run each singleton on one PostgreSQL session across replicas. The default local lease runs workers in the current process. A fatal error shuts down the RPC listener and the other workers.

Enable scheduled schema meters with `"meter":{"directory":"measurements","every_ms":86400000,"retry_ms":60000}`. The reader starts at launch, visits tenant IDs in pages, and runs again at UTC epoch-aligned boundaries. Each file records a single tenant, meter and measurement time. Failed deliveries retry with the original readings. An app can register its own singleton with `paydirt_store::meter::periodic` and use `Pass::run_with` to supply a tenant source, custom measurements and a billing sink.

Configure the primary listener in `app.json`. Under `serving`, `tls` takes PEM certificate and key paths. Adding `client_ca` requires a verified client certificate. An optional `web` listener serves the same router at its own `listen` address with an explicit array of allowed `origins` and optional TLS. Both listeners share `deadline_ms`, `max_message_bytes` and authorization. A top-level `rate` with positive `per_second` and `burst` charges authenticated tenants one unit per RPC or per AddMany item. Set `serving.otlp_traces_endpoint` to the collector's HTTP/protobuf trace URL (including `/v1/traces`) to export RPC spans with the configured service name and W3C trace parent.

Run `cargo test --test core` to exercise the assembled router in process. The `paydirt-test` fixture gives each test its own temporary SQLite database, a fixed clock, and a repeatable sequence of domain-correct IDs. Its Connect client sends real encoded requests through the application's layers. Use `assert_code` to verify a refused call.

Register a transaction-bound notification audience with `Store::with_notification_policy` on the native store or SQLite Worker. Its `Rows::get` reads pending application changes on the same connection. The policy supplies a stable reader, producer and topic grants. Set `notification_expiry_ms` in app.json to run the named singleton that erases notifications and receipts after the schema's `notify.keep.days`. The configured watch broker receives committed topic invalidations. Operators can process a single tenant or account namespace with `Store::expire_notifications`.

With entitlements enabled in the schema, configure the billing source in `app.json` as `"grant_source":{"url":"https://billing.example.org","bearer_env":"BILLING_TOKEN"}`. The native singleton calls the source's Connect GrantService. It waits for Watch readiness, consumes all List pages, applies stream updates and reconciles again on reconnect. The application continues to enforce its local grants while the source is unavailable. The optional `every_ms` and `retry_ms` settings control the reconciliation interval and initial retry delay.

Configure operator commands in `src/commands.rs`. A `Tree` supports application handlers, replacement, aliases, hidden commands and mounted protobuf packages. Custom handlers receive the selected Connect client and configuration. Register named output renderers under `formats` and table headings with JSON pointers under `columns`. Human output uses the IANA display zone from `--tz` or `APP_TZ`. JSON keeps UTC, and protojson keeps protobuf bytes in base64. Friendly JSON accepts nested row references and formats UUID fields according to the protobuf option.

Select externally readable entities in `resources.json`, for example `{"entities":["app.Robot","app.Cell"],"previous":true}`. This registers `payday.ResourceService/Watch` and enables the committing store to pack one public protobuf envelope per mutation. Subscribe before writing and wait for response headers. Consumers reconcile with List after disconnection. Enable the native `resource-cel` feature for boolean filters over public event fields, message type tests, field presence, equality, boolean operators, and string prefix, suffix or containment. The WASM build accepts an empty filter. Both builds apply the subscriber's Get grant and row scopes to each included image before delivery.

Native servers can configure `authentication` in app.json. An `oidc` entry takes an issuer URL and audience. It accepts signed RFC 9068 access tokens with RS256 or ES256 and follows provider discovery and key rotation. `introspection` takes a TokenService URL and the name of the environment variable holding this application's service credential. Map verified principals under `bindings`, using `oidc:SUBJECT`, `mtls:SHA256HEX`, or `remote:TENANT/ACTOR` with verified names or lowercase hexadecimal IDs. The usual local tenant, set, and method rules apply to these bindings. A client certificate requires a listener configured with a trusted client CA.

With `sessions:true`, an application-owned login RPC calls `Database::login_cookie` with the verified request context, the SDK store identity and a lifetime. Return the result in a Set-Cookie response header. Logout returns `Database::logout_cookie` the same way. The in-memory session store is process-local and revocable. The browser stores a Secure, HttpOnly, host-only cookie. Each SDK call's payday-identity header must agree with the session, including after another user signs in.

The sandbox exports `resourceHeaders(callback)` from client.js. Spread its result into a Connect call's options to observe the dRPC response headers before the first message. It works with the published dRPC Connect transport even for a stream that waits indefinitely for its first mutation.

Use `Database::with_presence` to enroll an application verifier for represented calls. The verifier receives the authenticated caller, its credential evidence, the requested subject, method, recording time and unary request. Verify the binding and return the person's frame, method grant, proof time and a revocable lease. Handler code can read `transport::presence::Parties` from the request context and require fresh proof separately for the person and the calling device. A direct-only RPC refuses representation. A stream checks its lease while idle and before forwarding each message.

Configure audit content in `trail.json`. The default `patch` setting records a protobuf patch. Choose `fields` or `value` globally with `default` or per fully qualified entity in `by`. Fields declared personal or secret are withheld from stored values. Enable `access` to record the subjects of calls and each stream message, and list export methods in `downloads` to require a base64-encoded UTF-8 reason in `payday-reason-bin`. Enable `reads` and grant the specific AccessService or AuditService methods to reviewers to mount their tenant-scoped Get and List endpoints. Downloads complete only after the record is durable.

With access recording enabled, a normal read queues the subjects whose personal fields the response carried. The server flushes them every second and retries a failed flush. Set `access_flush_ms` in `trail.json` to choose the interval, or to zero for synchronous reads. Downloads and named shell operators record reads before returning. To name a local operator, set `operator` in `app.json` to an object with `actor` and `tenant` UUIDs and an `address` such as `shell:operator@host`. Writes record their subjects once per owning tenant at commit.


The native operator runs trail maintenance with `APP trail '<request JSON>'`. Set `DATABASE_URL`, `PAYDIRT_ARCHIVE_DIR` to a directory dedicated to this database and `PAYDIRT_ARCHIVE_KEY` to a 32-byte hex key. Back up the key independently. Each request names a tenant UUID and an action. For example, `{"tenant":"<uuid>","action":"policy","policy":{"by":{"app.Robot":{"retain_ms":86400000,"destroy_ms":2592000000}},"held":["access"]}}` keeps robot audit rows online for one day and removes them after thirty, while holding Access records. An omitted retain window keeps the online row. Use `collect` for the scheduled pass, `inspect` to read inventory, `retire` for departure under the tenant's floors and `purge` for an explicit purge. Add `"dry_run":true` to preview selected row IDs. A hold on any named owner retains shared records.

Set the policy for `"tenant":"deployment"` to supply the default for tenants without their own policy. Run `APP trail-pass --dry-run` to preview each filing tenant and `APP trail-pass` to apply the policies. To remove archived rows of a single kind before an exclusive UTC cutoff, use `APP trail '{"tenant":"<uuid>","action":"purge_kind","kind":"robot","before":"2025-01-01T00:00:00Z"}'`. Read the resulting content-free receipts with `APP trail-receipts`. A receipt records the operation, time, tenant, kind, location and affected row count. Configured archive signers authenticate receipts. Pending receipts survive a process restart.

Applications can call `Transaction::forget_trail_in(tenant, &subjects)` inside their business transaction. A commit makes online redactions and archive erasure obligations durable together. A rollback reverses both. A later `retry` or maintenance pass performs the archive work.


`forget` takes a subject UUID. A hold leaves its erasure owed and `retry` settles that debt after release. Archive writes are encrypted and durably published before removing the online copy. A second transaction reclaims replaced objects. `export` takes a recipient name, operator and reason and writes an encrypted bundle into `PAYDIRT_EXPORT_DIR` using the independent `PAYDIRT_EXPORT_KEY`; the response identifies that bundle by digest.

Inside a Core RPC call `Database::begin_event` with the declared event protobuf and `transport::past::Options`. Reuse an event UUID for retries. `Begin::Done` means the earlier transaction committed and the handler can return its existing result. `Begin::Started` carries the transaction context for children and `Operation::facts` for valid-time changes. `past::detach` begins an independent cause in the same transaction. An event can precede the resource it creates. The framework checks an existing subject against the operation's tenant.

An application can assemble a `past::Catalog` of per-kind, per-language sentences. Its renderer receives the caller's context and timezone and can look up current display names through the application's scoped sinks. `redact_event` removes free-text reasons in the current transaction. The native operator runs `APP past-expire TENANT '{"window":{"keep_ms":86400000},"dry_run":true}'`. Holds, retained fact values, retained event kinds, live event pointers and causal ancestry affect the plan. The operator returns the affected event and fact IDs.

Inside a Core transaction, `Operation::children` writes child events and their facts in pages. Use `correct` or `retract` for one fact and `correct_event` or `retract_event` for all standing facts of an event. `Database::statements` accepts `Knowledge::Current`, `Knowledge::Ever` or `Knowledge::At`. `Database::state_at` reconstructs a protobuf message for a valid time and an optional knowledge time. A formatter can call `Reading::name` to resolve a live label through the caller's wall and include the named subject in Access.

A native deployment can set `"past_expiry":{"every_ms":3600000,"window":{"keep_ms":2592000000}}` in `app.json` to expire history for each tenant. Applications with their own time-bound rows can use `Store::expire_past_with` to clean them in the same transaction before the framework checks event references. When the application declares a `past.expire` payload with the cutoff and removal counts, a nonempty expiry records its receipt as an event.
